WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:07.720
What’s up guys?

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.370
Jared here with our third Wisecrack vlog.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:16.090
The internet has been ablaze revisiting the
complete shit show that was 2017’s Fyre

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.340
festival since two documentaries were released
on Hulu and Netflix.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.539
It seems that people can’t stop thinking
it, and I don’t think it’s simply because

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:25.960
we can’t look away from a train derailing.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.220
Fyre Festival is the perfect symbol for a
lot of things happening in our culture right

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.220
now.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.800
For those of you who DON’T know, a 25-year-old
“entrepreneur” named Billy McFarland and

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.650
Ja-Rule sold thousands of tickets to a music
festival in the Bahamas

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:44.860
by paying a bunch of instagram’s most popular
influencers to appear in a promotional video

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.240
promising an experience of a lifetime.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.890
Problem is, this festival had no chance of
happening.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:54.940
They promised an exclusive island once owned
by Pablo Escobar.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.730
They were given the backyard of a Sandals
resort.

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.150
They were promised Luxurious accommodations.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:05.509
They were given wet mattresses in overpopulated
FEMA tents - and a cruel interpretation of

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:06.689
Meatless Monday.

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:11.250
They were promised nights of models, drinking,
and dancing while listening to a legendary

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.250
lineup.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.729
They got a night locked in the Bahamas airport
hungover and starving.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:20.289
Turns out, Billy’s been defrauding investors
this whole time and gets sentenced to 6 years

00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:21.600
behind bars.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.069
People have criticized this event from every
angle- whether it’s the absurdity of Silicon

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:29.470
Valley Hustle culture,
Billy’s seeming sociopathy,

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:33.789
or FuckJerry’s dubious role in all this
there’s certainly no shortage of digital

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:35.909
ink spilled on the Fyre Festival.

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:41.509
But rather than thinking about Fyre Fest as
some weird aberration of modern culture, we

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:45.609
like to think of it as a definitive microcosm
of the world we live in.

00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:50.859
So welcome to this luxurious, curated experience
from the minds of Wisecrack as we take an

00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:54.130
exclusive journey into the bowels of Fyre
Fest.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:57.630
Let’s start off talking about the rich and
powerful.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:02.619
Since the establish of monarchy, and nobility
in general, people have flaunted their status

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:03.700
with objects.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:09.259
We’ve dug up pre-historic graves decked
out with ancient bling: precious metals, jewelry,

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:10.390
and pottery.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:14.940
Even with the decline of nobility in places
like Europe, nothing much changed.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.390
Your upstart “new money” merchants sought
to emulate the old rich by wearing the same

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.410
clothes and owning the same shit.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.990
Fast forward to today, where young people
are often, at least in part, rejecting this

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:27.990
logic.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.580
It’s not that people don’t want nice cars
or clothes anymore,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.730
but younger generations have put a newfound
premium on “experiences.”

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.540
And don’t get me wrong, this is great.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.110
There’s evidence out there that says you’ll
be happier spending a thousand bucks on a

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.660
vacation
than say, a 4k gaming monitor.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.000
Many of today’s trust fund kids don’t
necessarily flash their cash, but instead

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.570
pretend to be broke hipsters flaunting their
culture and worldliness.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:58.120
But among this generally positive trend, something
dark has emerged.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.070
Experiences themselves have become fake.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.000
Enter: Fyre Fest.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.920
Fyre Festival is the quintessential experience
to sell to rich pe…

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.640
I mean, uh,
PUNCTUATION: people of wealth

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.260
It’s the party of all parties.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.550
Something reserved for the best of the best.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:18.000
More than something to spend thousands of
dollars on, Fyre is an affirmation of who

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.620
you are, or who could you be.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.280
One can emulate the decadent lifestyle of
Pablo Escobar by partying on his private island

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.710
with the world’s most famous models.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:32.950
While this kind of experiential snobbery has
certain blossomed in the last decade, the

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.520
reason WHY was described by philosopher Jean
Baudrillard in 1968.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:42.130
As he wrote in his work “System of Objects”:
“Everywhere today, in fact, the ideology

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:45.890
of competition gives way to a ‘philosophy’
of self-fulfillment.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:51.540
In a more integrated society individuals no
longer compete for the possession of goods,

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.840
they actualize themselves in consumption.”

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.600
[System of objects]
Baudrillard was, in this case, talking about

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.930
material goods.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.980
People like Billy McFarland need to live in
a swanky penthouse and drive expensive cars

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.530
to give off the air that “Hey, I’m an
important guy you should trust with millions

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.530
of dollars.”

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:12.520
This kind of materialism-centric society,
as argued by fellow philosopher Guy Debord,

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:17.209
quickly transforms into a world where the
image of wealth overcomes the existence of

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:18.209
wealth.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.820
Billy flexing his money only mattered insofar
as he put up the appearance of wealth.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.670
As Debord said, “Being” devolves into
“having.”

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:32.720
But something else weird happens with this-
the logic of elitism tends to contradict itself.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.270
While Fyre puts on the image of being an exclusive
party for the world’s elite,

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:41.160
Billy admits in the documentary that the messaging
is meant for some average joe

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.210
PUNCTUATION: We're selling a pipe dream to
your average loser... you're average guy in

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.210
middle america.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.560
As Baudrillard writes : [“Advertising tells
us, at the same time: “buy this, for it

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.830
is like nothing else!”

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:58.940
(“The meat of the elite, the cigarette of
the happy few! etc); but also: “Buy this

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.210
because everyone else is using it!”]

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:05.720
In a world where the image of wealth is more
important than the wealth itself, Fyre Fest

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.760
is the perfect ticket.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.320
If experiences are the new ways in which the
rich communicate their supposed superiority,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.980
then Fyre lets even ordinary people act like
the super-rich.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:22.320
If it both “is” and “isn’t" for the
elite, we can also see how the experience

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:28.390
of something like a successful Fyre Fest or
Burning Man can be labeled artificial- for

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.139
it both “is” and “isn’t” real.

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:33.320
Now, I know that's a bit confusing, so let
me explain.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:38.060
In the novel “White Noise” by Don Delillo,
a family goes to visit the most photographed

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.670
barn in America.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:44.600
As they approach the barn, they are saturated
with images of the barn plastered on billboards,

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:45.760
and postcards.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.580
One character comments, “Nobody sees the
barn.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.590
Once you’ve seen the signs about the barn,
it becomes impossible to see the barn.”..

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:56.750
“[they’re] not here to capture an image,
we’re here to maintain one.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:01.250
Every photograph reinforces an aura…we’ve
agreed to be part of a collective perception.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:06.490
A religious experience in a way, like all
tourism… they’re taking pictures of taking

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.900
pictures.”

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.620
The elevated importance to which people treat
the barn has nothing to do with the barn itself,

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.450
but rather the proliferation of images OF
the barn.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.730
When you visit the most photographed barn
in the world, you’re not there to look at

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:26.000
the barn, you’re there to BE THERE- to be
one of the group whose collective reverence

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.380
elevates the barn to something more than just
a bunch of wood planks.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:35.750
Nobody gives a shit about the barn, they care
about taking part in the social aura that

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:37.949
deems the barn special.

00:06:37.949 --> 00:06:42.340
Ever been to a concert where people record
video of the performance instead of actually

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.950
watching the performance?

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.140
Driven to Joshua Tree just to get a profile
pic?

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:51.150
When you do these things, you’ve stopped
having an experience, and have started partaking

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.670
in an aura.

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:55.980
And this is the appeal of the Fyre Fest in
a nutshell.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:01.150
It was an event propelled entirely by a promotional
video that sold an aura.

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:05.640
And even if the festival was a total success,
the aura of the video would have mattered

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.010
more than the festival itself.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:12.610
You want to go to the Fyre festival because
you want to be part of maintaining the impossible

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:14.630
aura that was sold to you.

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:18.930
You would go and capture a single frame of
something beautiful and post it to instagram

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.630
with a hashtag that celebrated a once in a
lifetime experience.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:26.960
To thinkers like Baudrillard and Delillo,
it never really is about the experience.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.380
It’s about maintaining the aura of the experience.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.370
The experience always disappoints.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.880
The Bahamas are hot, and full of mosquitos,
and hanging around models will probably only

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.210
serve to distort your self-esteem.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:44.370
But that one instagram image will obscure
all those annoyances and function as a small,

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:49.660
but integral part in elevating the experience
to the sublime promised in the video.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:54.889
For Baudrillard, when we consume products,
we’re not consuming say, a bar of chocolate,

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:56.480
we’re consuming its meaning.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.680
And, for Baudrillard, the world has been completely
overcome by this logic: the symbols of things

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.080
have completely overcome the things themselves
PUNCTUATION: The promo video was perfectly

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:12.169
generic fantasia of what an instagram come
to life would be

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:17.160
And as a result, the best way to be a “part”
of Fyre Fest is not to have a fun time at

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.610
the festival,
but to telegraph to all of your followers

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:26.300
how you are having fun, even (or especially)
if it’s all bullshit.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:30.840
We must consume the festival’s meaning,
and then regurgitate that meaning for everyone

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.860
else
“And there are a lot of people in the millenial

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:37.580
generation that are interested in experiences
that are effectively like pretext for really

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:41.330
good instagrams”
And it’s not just music festivals and barns.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:45.630
Take the pop-up museum, in which a company
rents a space, fills it with objects, and

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:47.630
deems it an “experience."

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:52.710
This phenomenon spawned an amazing article
from writer Amanda Hess entitled “The Existential

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.060
Void of the Pop-Up Experience.”

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.779
Whether it’s the Museum of Ice Cream,
The Museum of Pizza,

00:08:57.779 --> 00:09:01.450
or the Color Factory,
Hess details how the act of “learning”

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:05.460
becomes “mandatory fun” at the risk of
ruining other people’s “good vibes”

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:10.700
of which they're going to pour into the composition
of their perfect ball-pit selfie.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:14.070
People don’t go to the museum of pizza to
learn about pizza,

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:19.180
they just go to take pictures with a pizza-shaped
guitar donated by Andrew WK, displayed in

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:22.779
a glass box for an extra air of importance.

00:09:22.779 --> 00:09:28.950
The real attraction is taking part in the
social media ritual that gives something significance.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:32.530
As Hess writes:
“The most that these spaces can offer is

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.820
the facsimile of traditional pleasures.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:39.980
They take nature and art and knowledge seeking,
flatten them into sight gags and stick them

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.060
to every stray surface.”

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:47.250
And if you consider this Fyre-themed pop-up
experience in LA, which also ended abruptly,

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.100
your head just might explode.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.100
Perhaps nothing speaks to this more than this
moment in the Netflix documentary.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.970
PUNCTUATION: “I was reading about a company
in Russia that now will sell people like three

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:02.070
hours to do a photoshoot on a private jet
that’s just parked on the tarmac.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:06.070
They never actually leave, but they get to
take the picture like looking out the window

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:10.050
and pretending they’re living this lifestyle
and that they’re successful.”

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:13.959
For some, any vague tie to reality can be
completely untethered.

00:10:13.959 --> 00:10:18.500
It’s not the experience itself, but the
image of that experience, that matters.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:23.070
That’s why it’s so poetic when Fyre compeletely
failed.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.700
The symbolic order comes crashing down
and we’re faced with the discrepancy between

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:32.380
the symbolic value and the actual value-
between luxury villas

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:33.691
and this
PUNCTUATION: my buddy pissed on a few of the

00:10:33.691 --> 00:10:37.370
beds
And that’s why this event has resonated

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:42.339
so much for us here at Wisecrack, aside from
one man’s very relatable quest for potable

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:43.339
water.

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:46.970
PUNCTUATION: And I got to his office, fully
prepared to suck his dick.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:51.020
That a festival that uses something as vapid
as instagram to sell itself ends up being

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:55.170
a complete failure
functions as the perfect representation of

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:59.970
the nothingness that lies underneath the symbols
and images we perpetuate.

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:05.800
Fyre is a sublime example of life hooked on
aura, and the obscene, shallow reality that

00:11:05.800 --> 00:12:07.510
lurks underneath it.

